**d. Why is the entropy change negative?**

The reaction converts two neutral molecules (DMEA and acetic acid) into two ions (DMEAH⁺ and acetate).  Even though the number of solute particles does not change, the formation of charged species introduces **strong solvation shells**: water molecules become ordered around the ions.  This ordering of the solvent (and the formation of ion‑pair hydrogen‑bonded structures) reduces the number of translational and rotational degrees of freedom, making the system more ordered.  Additionally, the neutral DMEA molecule loses some conformational flexibility upon protonation, further decreasing entropy.  Hence the net entropy change for the reaction is negative.
